        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which league does Eric Spokas play in?\n\nAnswer A: NCAA Division I\n  about: Eric Spokas\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Spokas played college basketball for Northeastern University from 1991 to 1993. During his time at Northeastern, he was a member of the university's men's basketball team, which competes in the NCAA Division I.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Israeli Premier League\n  about: Eric Spokas\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1995 to 2002\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Eric Spokas is a retired American-Israeli professional footballer who played in the Israeli Premier League from 1995 to 2002.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a potential Wikipedia entry that presents comprehensive evidence for the statement:\n\n**Eric Spokas**\n\nEric Spokas is a former American college basketball player who played in the NCAA Division I men's basketball league. **College Career**\n\nSpokas played college basketball for Northeastern University from 1991 to 1993. During his time at Northeastern, he was a member of the university's men's basketball team, which competes in the NCAA Division I. **NCAA Division I Men's Basketball**\n\nThe NCAA Division I men's basketball league is a college sports league in the United States that is governed by the National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA). It is the highest level of college basketball in the NCAA, and features 358 teams from colleges and universities across the country. **Evidence of Participation**\n\nAccording to Northeastern University's athletic website, Eric Spokas was a member of the men's basketball team from 1991 to 1993. [1] Additionally, Spokas is listed on the NCAA's website as a participant in the NCAA Division I men's basketball league during his time at Northeastern. [2]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] Northeastern University Athletics. (n.d.). Men's Basketball Roster. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/nuhuskies.com\\/sports\\/mens-basketball\\/roster>\n\n[2] National Collegiate Athletic Association. (n.d.). NCAA Division I Men's Basketball Participants.\nDocument 2: **Eric Spokas**\n\nEric Spokas is a retired American-Israeli professional footballer who played in the Israeli Premier League from 1995 to 2002. **Early Life and College Basketball Career**\n\nBorn on August 12, 1972, in Boston, Massachusetts, Spokas attended Northeastern University, where he played college basketball from 1991 to 1993. During his time at Northeastern, Spokas was a key player for the Huskies, helping the team reach the NCAA Tournament in 1992. [1]\n\n**Transition to Football**\n\nAfter his college basketball career, Spokas made the unexpected decision to pursue a career in football. He moved to Israel in 1994 to explore opportunities in the country's top division, the Israeli Premier League. **Israeli Premier League Career**\n\nSpokas signed with Hapoel Tel Aviv in 1995, making his professional debut on September 2, 1995, in a match against Maccabi Haifa. [2] Over the course of his seven-year career in the Israeli Premier League, Spokas played for several clubs, including Hapoel Tel Aviv, Beitar Jerusalem, and Maccabi Petah Tikva. He earned a reputation as a skilled midfielder, known for his vision and passing ability. **Notable Achievements**\n\nDuring his time in the Israeli Premier League, Spokas helped Hapoel Tel Aviv win the Toto Cup in 1999. [3] He also earned several call-ups to the Israel national football team, although he never made an official appearance. **Post-Playing Career**\n\nAfter retiring from professional football in 2002, Spokas went on to coach youth teams in Israel and the United States. He currently resides in Tel Aviv, where he works as a football analyst for Israeli sports media outlets. **References**\n\n[1] \"Northeastern University Athletics - 1992 NCAA Tournament\". Northeastern University Athletics. Retrieved 2023-02-15. [2] \"Hapoel Tel Aviv - Maccabi Haifa, 2-1\". Israeli Football Association. 1995-09-02. Retrieved 2023-02-15. [3] \"Hapoel Tel Aviv Wins Toto Cup\". Haaretz. 1999-02-10. Retrieved 2023-02-15.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
